 ### [Blur-Free Inconsistency Modeling](17175-blur-free-inconsistency-modeling.md)
-A scheme to mitigate multi-view inconsistency from single-image super-resolution models by using a learnable Inconsistency Modeling (IM) module and a Blur Proposal (BP) module to constrain the loss. It applies a pre-trained 2D super-resolution model (ISR) to low-resolution views, passes the output through the IM module (two residual blocks) to simulate inconsistency, and uses the BP module (a four-layer convolutional network) to predict per-pixel blur kernels $B_k$ from the detached high-resolution rendered image. The blur kernel size $K$ is set to 5 for 4× super-resolution tasks, and the weight for the D-SSIM loss term $\beta$ is set to 0.2.
+A scheme to mitigate multi-view inconsistency from single-image super-resolution models by using a learnable Inconsistency Modeling (IM) module and a Blur Proposal (BP) module to constrain the loss [G]. It applies a pre-trained 2D super-resolution model (ISR) to low-resolution views, passes the output through the IM module (two residual blocks) to simulate inconsistency, and uses the BP module (a four-layer convolutional network) to predict per-pixel blur kernels $B_k$ from the detached high-resolution rendered image [G]. The blur kernel size $K$ is set to 5 for 4× super-resolution tasks, and the weight for the D-SSIM loss term $\beta$ is set to 0.2 [G].
 
 ## Background
-The field began with Zhou et al., who introduced the first CNN-based approach to volume super-resolution by adapting the SRCNN architecture to 3D patches from medical CT volumes [S1, unofficial]. Subsequent developments included GAN-based methods like SSRTVD for time-varying data, which used dual discriminators for spatial and temporal coherence [S1, unofficial]. Diffusion-based approaches, such as CD-TVD, later extended the family to scientific volumes using contrastive pre-training [10411-contrastive-diffusion-for-volume-super-resolution-cd-tvd.md].
